## What is phoXiv?

phoXiv intends to be the most comprehensive archive of the high school physics olympiad problems. It aims to have the most complete and up-to-date set of files for the canonical physics olympiads, and also serve as an introduction to the lesser-known olympiads. At the time of writing, the set of files for [IPhO](/olympiads/ipho) and [APhO](/olympiads/apho) are (as far as I can tell) the most complete out of any website.

## How does this compare to other archives?

Probably the most popular physics olympiad archive is Olimpicos. And, to be honest, phoXiv is largely the same as olimpicos in terms of olympiad coverage! But this is to be expected, as Olimpicos scraped phoXiv and copied over all of our files, even those that aren't credited in the footer. Still, phoXiv has files that Olimpicos forgot to copy over, so you should still stick with us :)

Unlike most archives, phoXiv has an open contributing system. Anyone can contribute (as long as you drop me an email first), so if you have something that's not on here, you can add it yourself!


## Why?

This website was made out of pure frustration. When I was studying for IPhO, I was extremely annoyed by the fact that the documentation for physics olympiads were so terrible. It seemed impossible to find somewhere that contained all the problems I wanted. When I was doing APhO problems, I found that the commonly-used olimpicos APhO archive was both outdated and did not have experimental problems. Meanwhile, for IPhO problems, the official website has not been updated since 2019. In fact, the official website literally has Lorem ipsum on the front page.

<enhanced:img alt="IPhO Lorem Ipsum" src="#lib/assets/images/ipho-clowning.png" class="w-full sm:w-3/4 mx-auto" />


<p class="text-center -translate-y-4 text-muted-foreground">wow.</p>

I eventually found [Kevin Zhou's handouts](https://knzhou.github.io/#physics-olympiad-handouts), which thankfully contained everything I needed, but I found the dropbox folder to be difficult to navigate on mobile. So I decided to make a website to solve all of these problems once and for all. Also, I kind of felt like learning how to make a website. Now, our IPhO archive is more up-to-date than the [official archive](https://www.ipho-new.org/documentations/), more comprehensive than the commonly-used [olimpicos IPhO archive](https://ipho.olimpicos.net/) while being arguably more convenient to use than either one!

## Who?

The vast majority of this website, from collating files to writing the backend and frontend code, is done solely by me. However, an increasing number of people are requesting to contribute, which is very nice! Check out the [team page](/team) to see everyone who has helped out.


## Is this sustainable?

I have mentioned above that many physics olympiad websites have been lost to time, like the IPhO official website which is practically dead since 2019. This is inevitable due to how decentralised physics olympiads are. Many of the students or team leaders who make such websites become too busy to maintain them. I will be personally maintaining this site for the forseeable future (at least the next 2 years), but after that, I can't be completely sure I'll have time for this. To prepare for that eventuality, I have open-sourced this website to encourage more people to contribute, and written detailed documentation. One of the few physics olympiad websites that remains up-to-date is [ipho-unofficial](https://ipho-unofficial.org/). I hope that, just like for ipho-unofficial, making this website open-source and providing detailed documentation will be enough to keep this project alive.

## Sounds cool! How do I contribute?

Make an account and [email me](mailto:apochrome@proton.me) to let me know you're interested in contributing. If you have any feature requests or suggestions, email me, create a [GitHub](https://github.com/apchrme/phoxiv) issue, or send a message in [#phoxiv-discussion](https://discord.com/channels/1477813246051356842/1489615518544433223) on the Discord server (if you can't see that link, you haven't joined the [server](https://discord.gg/SNBDY5nsgf)).


